# Target C — exact exceptional-face closure corank equals pure contact stress dimension

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of `target_c_train_closure_rank_and_bracing_2026-09-12.md`; theorem authority unchanged.

The closure-rank theorem has a sharper exact form. Let `C_face` be the `2f x T`
exceptional-face closure matrix defined in the companion note, and let

\[
\sigma_C:=\dim\ker R_C^T
\]

be the dimension of the pure contact self-stress space on the nonisolated positive
contact core. Then

\[
\boxed{
2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}=\sigma_C.
}
\tag{1}
\]

Canonical mixed rigidity then gives `sigma_C<=s_Z`, recovering and conceptually
strengthening

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}\ge2f-s_Z.
\]

Thus every exact linear dependency among the two-coordinate exceptional-face closure
conditions is literally the dual shadow of a pure contact self-stress. The defect
faces do not have an independent hidden source of closure-row degeneracy.

---

## 1. Contact-flex dimension from the train closure matrix

Use the notation of the companion theorem. The audited stopped-train decomposition has
`T` train variables. The companion Lemma 4.1 proves that, modulo translations, the
contact infinitesimal flex space is `ker C_face`. The constant train angular-rate vector
is global rotation. Therefore the non-Euclidean contact-flex dimension is

\[
\boxed{
d_C=T-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}-1.
}
\tag{2}
\]

This statement permits bridges, articulation vertices, singleton trains, and arbitrary
bounded nonquadrilateral faces.

---

## 2. The same dimension from the contact rigidity matrix

Let `R_C` be the contact rigidity matrix on the same `v` nonisolated core vertices and
`e` actual contact edges. By rank-nullity,

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_C=e-\sigma_C.
\]

The contact infinitesimal-flex space modulo Euclidean motions therefore has dimension

\[
\begin{aligned}
d_C
&=(2v-3)-\operatorname{rank}R_C\\
&=2v-3-e+\sigma_C.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

Euler for the connected plane contact graph gives

\[
v-e+(R+f+1)=2,
\]

so

\[
2v-3-e
=e-2R-2f-1.
\]

The audited train identity is `T=e-2R`. Hence

\[
\boxed{
d_C=T-2f-1+\sigma_C.
}
\tag{4}
\]

Compare (2) and (4). The `T-1` terms cancel, leaving the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}=\sigma_C.
}
\]

No KKT positivity is needed for this equality.

---

## 3. Mixed rigidity bounds the pure-contact stress space

Canonical core-only ZR/ZF gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}=2v-3
\]

and

\[
e+h=2v-2+s_Z.
\]

Therefore the full signed equilibrium-stress space on the actual contact and diameter
rows has dimension

\[
(e+h)-(2v-3)=s_Z+1.
\tag{5}
\]

Every pure contact self-stress `lambda in ker R_C^T` extends to a mixed stress
`(lambda,0_H)`. These form a `sigma_C`-dimensional subspace of the mixed stress space.

The normalized KKT equilibrium stress has a nonzero diameter part; in the fully
force-visible relative-interior normalization every actual diameter coefficient is in
fact positive. Hence that KKT stress is not in the pure-contact subspace. Consequently

\[
\sigma_C+1\le s_Z+1,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\sigma_C\le s_Z.}
\tag{6}
\]

Combining (1) and (6) gives

\[
\boxed{
0\le2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}
=\sigma_C
\le s_Z.
}
\tag{7}
\]

In particular, `s_Z=0` forces both

\[
\ker R_C^T=\{0\}
\]

and

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_{\rm face}=2f.
\]

---

## 4. Why this is useful for the all-regime corridor route

A macroscopic Type-II exceptional face is proof-only known to collapse to two
near-geodesic, almost projectively one-directional boundary routes. Such a face has a
closure block that can be extremely ill-conditioned while retaining exact rank two.
Equation (1) says that any **exact** loss of closure rank is not a local geometric
accident: it creates a pure contact self-stress. Across all faces, only `s_Z` such
scalar losses are available.

This reframes the remaining Route-B problem sharply:

> convert the physical near-rank-one corridor blocks into either an exact row
> dependency, or a quantitative small-singular-value direction whose associated
> train-angle flex contradicts diameter minimality before the `s_Z` mixed-stress
> budget can absorb it.

The theorem does not itself supply that quantitative conditioning estimate.

---

## 5. Scope audit

- All rigidity matrices are on the nonisolated positive-contact core only.
- Contact-isolated source points are retained in the full source and are not inserted
  into `R_C` or `R_{C union H}`.
- The exact identity uses only planar Euler, the audited stopped-train decomposition,
  and elementary rigidity rank-nullity.
- The inequality `sigma_C<=s_Z` uses only the canonical mixed rank and the existence of
  one KKT equilibrium stress with nonzero diameter part.
- No response theorem, carrier constant, or asymptotic limiting argument is used.

**Disposition:** proof-only exact structural identity. C-II remains open.
